{
  "dom_id": "step-q213065",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & ≡13\\cdot 9+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡13\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 140 & ≡15\\cdot 9+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\cdot 0+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\cdot 140 & ≡3\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=6.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
